    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a braise that takes an afternoon and pays you back all week. Like most braises, it is better on day three than it was the day you made it.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePork shoulder instead of pork belly is the budget and health move.\u003c/strong\u003e Belly is traditional and it is also mostly fat you are paying for. Shoulder braises just as tender and carries far more protein per dollar.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eFried onions replace the fried shallots.\u003c/strong\u003e Traditional lu rou fan starts with crispy fried shallots, which are pricey. Yellow onions fried golden in the same oil get you most of the way there.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eFive spice powder is the whole soul of the dish.\u003c/strong\u003e One small jar lasts a year of batches. This is not the ingredient to substitute or skip.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eEggs braised in the sauce are cheap protein.\u003c/strong\u003e Hard boil them, peel them, and let them sit in the braise. They soak up the flavor and add real protein for very little money."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Hand dice the pork shoulder into small pieces rather than grinding it. The texture of lu rou fan comes from distinct little cubes, not a paste.",
                    "Hard boil the eggs, cool them, and peel them so they are ready to go into the braise.",
                    "Heat the vegetable oil in a heavy pot and fry the sliced onions until they turn deep golden and crisp at the edges, then lift most of them out and set them aside.",
                    "Brown the diced pork in that same oil until the pieces have color and the fat starts to render.",
                    "Add the garlic and the sugar and stir until the sugar melts and coats everything in a dark glaze, which takes only a minute or two and is the step that makes this dish look right.",
                    "Pour in the soy sauce and the chicken broth, add the sliced mushrooms, the five spice powder, the black pepper, and the salt, then return the fried onions.",
                    "Add the peeled eggs, cover, and simmer very low until the pork is falling apart. Cook the rice and blanch the broccoli near the end so both are fresh."
                ],
